1. INTERVIEW READY ANSWERS
======================================================================

Q1. What is Sorting?

Answer:
Sorting is the process of arranging data in ascending or descending order. It improves searching, data processing, and organization.

Why do we need Sorting?

• Required before Binary Search.
• Easy to find minimum and maximum values.
• Makes searching faster.
• Helps in data analysis and reporting.
• Used in databases and search engines.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. What is Bubble Sort?

Answer:
Bubble Sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that repeatedly compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order.

After every pass,
→ The largest unsorted element reaches its correct position.

Time Complexity

Best
→ O(n) (Optimized Bubble Sort)

Average
→ O(n²)

Worst
→ O(n²)

Space
→ O(1)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Why is it called Bubble Sort?

Answer:
After every pass, the largest unsorted element moves to the end of the array, similar to how an air bubble rises to the surface.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Advantages of Bubble Sort

Answer:

• Very simple implementation.
• Easy to understand.
• Good for very small datasets.
• Optimized version can detect an already sorted array.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Disadvantages of Bubble Sort

Answer:

• O(n²) in average and worst case.
• Large number of swaps.
• Inefficient for large datasets.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q6. What is Selection Sort?

Answer:
Selection Sort repeatedly finds the minimum element from the unsorted portion of the array and places it at its correct position.

After every pass,
→ The smallest element reaches its correct position.

Time Complexity

Best
→ O(n²)

Average
→ O(n²)

Worst
→ O(n²)

Space
→ O(1)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Why is Best Case of Selection Sort still O(n²)?

Answer:
Even if the array is already sorted, Selection Sort still scans the remaining unsorted part to find the minimum element. Therefore, the number of comparisons remains the same.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Advantages of Selection Sort

Answer:

• Performs fewer swaps.
• Simple implementation.
• O(1) extra space.
• Useful when swapping is expensive.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q9. Disadvantages of Selection Sort

Answer:

• Always O(n²).
• Not adaptive.
• Slow for large datasets.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q10. What is Insertion Sort?

Answer:
Insertion Sort builds the sorted part of the array one element at a time. It picks the next element as a key, shifts larger elements to the right, and inserts the key into its correct position.

Time Complexity

Best
→ O(n)

Average
→ O(n²)

Worst
→ O(n²)

Space
→ O(1)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q11. Why is Insertion Sort efficient for nearly sorted arrays?

Answer:
If the array is already sorted or nearly sorted, very few elements need to be shifted. Therefore, only a small number of comparisons and shifts are performed, making the best-case time complexity O(n).

------------------------------------------------------------

Q12. Advantages of Insertion Sort

Answer:

• Efficient for small datasets.
• Efficient for nearly sorted arrays.
• Stable sorting algorithm.
• O(1) extra space.
• Online algorithm (can sort elements as they arrive).

------------------------------------------------------------

Q13. Disadvantages of Insertion Sort

Answer:

• O(n²) in average and worst case.
• Not suitable for large datasets.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q14. Bubble Sort vs Selection Sort vs Insertion Sort

Feature               Bubble           Selection         Insertion

Working               Adjacent Swap    Select Minimum    Shift & Insert

Best Case             O(n)*            O(n²)             O(n)

Average               O(n²)            O(n²)             O(n²)

Worst                 O(n²)            O(n²)             O(n²)

Space                 O(1)             O(1)              O(1)

Stable                Yes              No                Yes

Swaps                 Many             Very Few          Few

Adaptive              Yes*             No                Yes

(*Optimized Bubble Sort)

